WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:01.080
(spirited orchestral music)

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Narrator] This is the
aluminum ball challenge.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:06.690
- We are doing the aluminum
foil ball challenge.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.280
- [Narrator] It swept
the internet. (knocking)

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.950
It was pretty much people making smooth,

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.400
shiny balls out of aluminum foil.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.630
It originated in Japan, but
the story doesn't end there.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.920
It gets more meta, because this challenge

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.840
actually came from another
tradition in Japan;

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.880
and in true Japanese fashion,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.133
that tradition is pretty spectacular.

00:00:27.201 --> 00:00:28.034
(soft piano music)

00:00:28.034 --> 00:00:29.990
Dorodango is a Japanese art form

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.980
in which earth and water are molded

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.440
to create a delicate, shiny sphere.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.520
That's a nice way of saying

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.800
that these are very fancy mud balls.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.210
And when it comes to
making dorodango in Japan,

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.764
Kiyoko Miki is one of the best.

00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:45.681
(foreign language)

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.920
The process, condensed, goes like this:

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.330
Get a clay-based mud;

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.283
mix it with hay; this helps
keep it from falling apart.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.537
Roll it into a sphere, and
leave it to dry in the sun.

00:01:25.537 --> 00:01:28.454
(foreign language)

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.170
And the story doesn't end here,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.080
because where there is
something beautiful,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.420
there is a competition
to prove who is the best.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.313
And Kiyoko is a judge.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.532
This is the annual dorodango
tournament in Tokoname, Japan.

00:02:03.532 --> 00:02:06.449
(foreign language)

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.310
This year, contestants from all over Japan

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.063
have come to showcase their work.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.770
It looks like they've got a winner.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.920
So what does a winning
dorodango look like?

00:02:55.055 --> 00:02:56.183
Hmm, very shiny.

00:02:57.201 --> 00:02:59.830
(foreign language)

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.970
Colored, plain, large, small,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.300
the dorodango is not a one-look pony.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.320
It exemplifies so much
of Japanese culture:

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.920
patience, perfection, and the pursuit

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.200
of beauty in everyday things.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.200
And they're also pretty cool to look at.

